    "short_note": "WEB says Jesus loved his own “to the end.” Greek εἰς τέλος can describe love continuing to its endpoint. In this scene, the phrase also gains climactic force as Jesus moves toward betrayal and death, but the words alone don’t require two separate meanings.",
    "full_note_paragraphs": [
      "WEB’s “to the end” closely reflects εἰς τέλος, a phrase that can describe continuation toward an endpoint.",
      "In John 13, Jesus’ love continues through the events now beginning, including service, betrayal, denial, and death. That context can give the phrase a sense of love carried through to completion.",
      "The safest reading is not that τέλος independently means both “end” and “fullest extent.” The temporal force is clear, while the narrative supplies the climactic effect."
    ],
    "interpretive_limits": [
      "The phrase alone doesn’t prove that temporal and qualitative senses are equally encoded.",
      "The climactic force comes from the surrounding narrative, not from τέλος in isolation."
    ],
